er conclusions to be justified rather than inferred from examples alone. As the final topic in this module, logic gates carries the completed idea into logic minimization."}]}, {"id": "c8189f83-dafb-4b3e-9663-0fc9b420217f", "type": "topic", "title": "Logic minimization", "description": "This module builds directly on boolean algebra and develops logic minimization. It belongs here because its methods and distinctions are prerequisites for combinational blocks.", "children": [{"id": "e6da7333-39f2-4663-8cd2-b237b15f656a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Karnaugh maps", "children": [], "description": "Karnaugh maps gives logic minimization a reusable representation for organizing assumptions, variables, and relationships before analysis begins. It appears before implicants because that next topic assumes this starting point."}, {"id": "65d75c5b-7714-4c6d-9ba0-d2a81477b568", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Implicants", "children": [], "description": "Implicants identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within logic minimization. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After karnaugh maps, implicants supplies what is needed to address don’t-cares."}, {"id": "78e5a5a3-4d05-4481-988c-3cd67338f76e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Don’t-cares", "children": [], "description": "Don’t-cares identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within logic minimization. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After implicants, don’t-cares supplies what is needed to address multi-level tradeoffs."}, {"id": "f45fd714-2771-4758-8fa5-984ceac1d70c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Multi-level tradeoffs", "children": [], "description": "Multi-level tradeoffs identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within logic minimization. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. As the final topic in this module, multi-level tradeoffs carries the completed idea into combinational blocks."}]}, {"id": "a7bc43f8-1ee2-44c4-bc6f-7339ab0d6114", "type": "topic", "title": "Combinational blocks", "description": "This module builds directly on logic minimization and develops combinational blocks. It belongs here because its methods and distinctions are prerequisites for arithmetic circuits.", "children": [{"id": "1b12ca11-4c1a-4e71-b707-cd38161576c4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Multiplexers", "children": [], "description": "Multiplexers identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within combinational blocks. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. It appears before decoders because that next topic assumes this starting point."}, {"id": "e89e6e0a-5e7c-4645-a45c-98e0116d5fc9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Decoders", "children": [], "description": "Decoders identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within combinational blocks. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After multiplexers, decoders supplies what is needed to address encoders."}, {"id": "fdc03c88-fa48-4200-ae10-513eeddf65fc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Encoders", "children": [], "description": "Encoders identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within combinational blocks. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After decoders, encoders supplies what is needed to address comparators."}, {"id": "97717610-4b38-4413-9900-27a41aa81b95", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparators", "children": [], "description": "Comparators identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within combinational blocks. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After encoders, comparators supplies what is needed to address parity."}, {"id": "5e060ad7-98a0-44b4-8449-908002650259", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Parity", "children": [], "description": "Parity identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within combinational blocks. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. As the final topic in this module, parity carries the completed idea into arithmetic circuits."}]}, {"id": "406ed0ec-d252-4830-876f-6bc420f6a9e4", "type": "topic", "title": "Arithmetic circuits", "description": "This module builds directly on combinational blocks and develops arithmetic circuits. It belongs here because its methods and distinctions are prerequisites for storage elements.", "children": [{"id": "fd6ac89d-437e-42a8-a10c-7f51e56fa869", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Adders", "children": [], "description": "Adders identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within arithmetic circuits. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. It appears before subtraction because that next topic assumes this starting point."}, {"id": "d7c3b03b-490c-41b9-bbba-27ab401a5b78", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Subtraction", "children": [], "description": "Subtraction identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within arithmetic circuits. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After adders, subtraction supplies what is needed to address alu."}, {"id": "3b8e9739-a4a0-486a-b00b-e602c247b623", "type": "subtopic", "title": "ALU", "children": [], "description": "ALU identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within arithmetic circuits. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After subtraction, alu supplies what is needed to address multiplication."}, {"id": "6943ea2f-6e0b-4159-a7f5-0b95d0afd4c3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Multiplication", "children": [], "description": "Multiplication identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within arithmetic circuits. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After alu, multiplication supplies what is needed to address overflow."}, {"id": "c4ac6fcf-2952-40e2-b341-58d937046315", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Overflow", "children": [], "description": "Overflow identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within arithmetic circuits. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. As the final topic in this module, overflow carries the completed idea into storage elements."}]}, {"id": "7d59b038-e914-476f-b11b-abc1cf69f90b", "type": "topic", "title": "Storage elements", "description": "This module builds directly on arithmetic circuits and develops storage elements. It belongs here because its methods and distinctions are prerequisites for finite-state machines.", "children": [{"id": "8c02e381-1248-4d56-8bf2-57706fbc347e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Latches", "children": [], "description": "Latches identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within storage elements. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. It appears before flip-flops because that next topic assumes this starting point."}, {"id": "be3b7a75-b2e0-4fb5-95fb-63c8199942be", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Flip-flops", "children": [], "description": "Flip-flops identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within storage elements. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After latches, flip-flops supplies what is needed to address registers."}, {"id": "40c37aeb-acf4-402c-ac41-5303f611c11e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Registers", "children": [], "description": "Registers identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within storage elements. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After flip-flops, registers supplies what is needed to address counters."}, {"id": "76761bcb-675f-457e-ba7c-5adcde075f92", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Counters", "children": [], "description": "Counters identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within storage elements. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After registers, counters supplies what is needed to address clock enables."}, {"id": "d0f22b53-f69b-43a2-af6b-87e021a78617", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Clock enables", "children": [], "description": "Clock enables identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within storage elements. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. As the final topic in this module, clock enables carries the completed idea into finite-state machines."}]}, {"id": "bdd2a141-ad07-41d1-90cd-a6b6b3c759b7", "type": "topic", "title": "Finite-state machines", "description": "This module builds directly on storage elements and develops finite-state machines. It belongs here because its methods and distinctions are prerequisites for timing analysis.", "children": [{"id": "eb01f2b6-a489-43de-bf2e-194f680e730e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Moore/Mealy models", "children": [], "description": "Moore/Mealy models gives finite-state machines a reusable representation for organizing assumptions, variables, and relationships before analysis begins. It appears before state encoding because that next topic assumes this starting point."}, {"id": "f6b094f7-0b7f-40e0-a398-0f3887af88a8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "State encoding", "children": [], "description": "State encoding is an executable part of finite-state machines; its correctness, cost, and failure modes recur in later problems and implementations. After moore/mealy models, state encoding supplies what is needed to address sequence detectors."}, {"id": "198305c9-7f95-4033-9669-15b91bb70f1c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sequence detectors", "children": [], "description": "Sequence detectors identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within finite-state machines. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. As the final topic in this module, sequence detectors carries the completed idea into timing analysis."}]}, {"id": "82bbfbd5-dc52-4ad8-b608-91409fd51467", "type": "topic", "title": "Timing analysis", "description": "This module builds directly on finite-state machines and develops timing analysis. It belongs here because its methods and distinctions are prerequisites for asynchronous boundaries.", "children": [{"id": "65aa2052-03ee-44ed-a6b0-7d23448b4147", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Propagation/contamination delay", "children": [], "description": "Propagation/contamination delay identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within timing analysis. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. It appears before setup/hold because that next topic assumes this starting point."}, {"id": "3c1d28d9-ecd2-4b93-8871-e7abbc865aba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Setup/hold", "children": [], "description": "Setup/hold identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within timing analysis. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After propagation/contamination delay, setup/hold supplies what is needed to address critical paths."}, {"id": "e92ee559-5b87-4077-862b-482a83b07412", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Critical paths", "children": [], "description": "Critical paths identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within timing analysis. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After setup/hold, critical paths supplies what is needed to address skew."}, {"id": "d84a1d3a-57d7-45cb-b26f-e0be53c2efce", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Skew", "children": [], "description": "Skew identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within timing analysis. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. As the final topic in this module, skew carries the completed idea into asynchronous boundaries."}]}, {"id": "feabbbd1-5de4-4806-b192-dba8f6514bb3", "type": "topic", "title": "Asynchronous boundaries", "description": "This module builds directly on timing analysis and develops asynchronous boundaries. It belongs here because its methods and distinctions are prerequisites for memory systems.", "children": [{"id": "c85f7b58-1796-4f53-89bc-240e87959e58", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Metastability", "children": [], "description": "Metastability makes the validity limits of asynchronous boundaries explicit, showing when an otherwise correct method or system can become unreliable. It appears before synchronizers because that next topic assumes this starting point."}, {"id": "7520b41e-94ab-430d-ad5a-27e7c4e08a45", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Synchronizers", "children": [], "description": "Synchronizers identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within asynchronous boundaries. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After metastability, synchronizers supplies what is needed to address debouncing."}, {"id": "1f60450e-740c-4f3f-9b20-159af8ba7381", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Debouncing", "children": [], "description": "Debouncing identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within asynchronous boundaries. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After synchronizers, debouncing supplies what is needed to address handshakes."}, {"id": "fa9a3198-42c8-49ec-8c3e-a2e2ca55f3d9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Handshakes", "children": [], "description": "Handshakes identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within asynchronous boundaries. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. As the final topic in this module, handshakes carries the completed idea into memory systems."}]}, {"id": "c2bacdbb-b143-44ec-9fe9-29d7e0f54a0e", "type": "topic", "title": "Memory systems", "description": "This module builds directly on asynchronous boundaries and develops memory systems. It belongs here because its methods and distinctions are prerequisites for hdl and synthesis.", "children": [{"id": "c1d589ca-e5b3-4bb4-9b79-5a278aaa351d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "ROM/RAM", "children": [], "description": "ROM/RAM identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within memory systems. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. It appears before register files because that next topic assumes this starting point."}, {"id": "28d9a007-1b78-4b82-8b45-6859aae4e160", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Register files", "children": [], "description": "Register files identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within memory systems. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After rom/ram, register files supplies what is needed to address address decoding."}, {"id": "5e492f69-3cdb-45e6-b85c-7c2efae64ecf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Address decoding", "children": [], "description": "Address decoding is an executable part of memory systems; its correctness, cost, and failure modes recur in later problems and implementations. After register files, address decoding supplies what is needed to address memory timing."}, {"id": "3add1896-0652-48c1-bbad-09e7f03f8bad", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Memory timing", "children": [], "description": "Memory timing identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within memory systems. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. As the final topic in this module, memory timing carries the completed idea into hdl and synthesis."}]}, {"id": "68c25e2c-3b3a-4ab0-914b-57a3a144f0a0", "type": "topic", "title": "HDL and synthesis", "description": "This module builds directly on memory systems and develops hdl and synthesis. It belongs here because its methods and distinctions are prerequisites for verification and system design.", "children": [{"id": "e4a6c877-2c1c-44e2-b3a1-91caddba2874", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Synthesizable constructs", "children": [], "description": "Synthesizable constructs identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within hdl and synthesis. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. It appears before hierarchy because that next topic assumes this starting point."}, {"id": "9b5b6008-666a-4406-a5b9-86c79eca5c56", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Hierarchy", "children": [], "description": "Hierarchy identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within hdl and synthesis. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After synthesizable constructs, hierarchy supplies what is needed to address parameterization."}, {"id": "ed7a75c7-ffdf-4818-a2b7-e91d94c2c7f5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Parameterization", "children": [], "description": "Parameterization identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within hdl and synthesis. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After hierarchy, parameterization supplies what is needed to address inferred hardware."}, {"id": "aa0d8b95-ee9d-482f-a820-32d219694f1b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Inferred hardware", "children": [], "description": "Inferred hardware identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within hdl and synthesis. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. As the final topic in this module, inferred hardware carries the completed idea into verification and system design."}]}, {"id": "8b924302-929f-4105-91b9-a8907e4a3787", "type": "topic", "title": "Verification and system design", "description": "This culminating module draws on hdl and synthesis and develops verification and system design. It belongs last because it requires the earlier tools to be selected, combined, and evaluated rather than used one at a time.", "children": [{"id": "def41c2a-b5ff-40bb-9ed3-4f620d4d2097", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Testbenches", "children": [], "description": "Testbenches identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within verification and system design. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. It appears before assertions because that next topic assumes this starting point."}, {"id": "f8b8b498-1a24-4cd6-a1e2-740555c27497", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Assertions", "children": [], "description": "Assertions identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within verification and system design. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After testbenches, assertions supplies what is needed to address coverage."}, {"id": "ab79b97f-da90-466a-bdbf-96975c3b9f9d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Coverage", "children": [], "description": "Coverage identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within verification and system design. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After assertions, coverage supplies what is needed to address controller–datapath integration."}, {"id": "a0b5c701-32db-46c2-8b03-29a41e9c854d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Controller–datapath integration", "children": [], "description": "Controller–datapath integration connects verification and system design to observable evidence and defines what a valid measurement or interpretation must support. As the final course topic, controller–datapath integration requires ideas from the complete path to be integrated and transferred."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$dldzzjs$::jsonb,
